{"timestamp":"2026-07-14T12:06:09.741Z","ordinal":11,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"<skill>\n<name>think-of-everything</name>\n<path>/Users/zenray/.codex/skills/think-of-everything/SKILL.md</path>\n---\nname: think-of-everything\ndescription: Run a systematic impact and premortem pass on any decision or change in any domain — code, architecture, product, business, life/admin, travel, purchases, health, learning, art/3D/creative. Reads the request four ways (literal, inverse, adjacent, reframed), verifies the real facts instead of guessing from memory (grep the codebase, read the actual documents and numbers, search official sources), checks a 12-axis impact surface (existing state, conflicts, limits and deadlines, security/legal, partial failure, backward compatibility, scale, recurring cost, observability, edge cases, checks, reversibility), runs a domain-specific premortem, then delivers at most 5 ranked risks with exact proof and a concrete verification each, ending in a GO / NO-GO verdict at paid-specialist precision, never generic. Use whenever the user says \"pense à tout\", \"think of everything\", \"qu'est-ce que j'ai oublié\", \"what am I missing\", asks what a change or decision might break, or wants a premortem, dependency check, or impact analysis. Also trigger proactively, without the phrase, before shipping anything touching shared state, storage or quotas, concurrency, auth or permissions, payments, a public API/route — or any decision that is expensive, irreversible, or deadline-bound.\n---\n\n# Think of Everything (Pense à Tout)\n\nSurface the FEW risks that matter on ANY decision — code, business, life, art — each proven against the real facts, each with a concrete check, ending in a decision. 40 generic checklist items is a failure; 5 proven, ranked risks is a success. This pass answers the question asked, its opposite, and the ones nobody asked, then verifies against reality instead of memory.\n\n**Specialist rule (anti-generic), applies to every output line:** write at the level of the expert who gets paid to review exactly this — immigration lawyer, senior rigger, tax advisor, DB architect. Every kept risk must contain at least one exact value from the real context (number, date, name, path, price, spec) verified this session. A risk that could be pasted unchanged into someone else's situation is generic: sharpen it or cut it.\n\n## Step 0: Classify and scale (two lines)\n\n- Domain + the specialist to embody + type: (a) action to take, (b) existing thing to audit, (c) pure decision, nothing built yet.\n- For (b), the source of truth is the actual artifact (`git diff`, the contract, the scene file), never its description.\n- Trivial, cheap and reversible case: say so, jump to Step 4 + verdict, 5 output lines max. The full pass is for anything expensive, irreversible, deadline-bound, or touching shared state, money, auth, public APIs.\n\n## Step 1: Read the request four ways (one line each)\n\n1. **Literal**: restate exactly what was asked.\n2. **Inverse**: what if the premise is false? What single condition breaks this? And what does NOT doing it cost?\n3. **Adjacent**: the 2-3 questions the specialist would ask next, unprompted. Parallel questions, not follow-ups.\n4. **Reframed**: the clearest version of this request. If it differs from the literal one, answer that too.\n\n## Step 2: Verify the real facts (never from memory)\n\n**Evidence rule, the core of this skill: every claim cites its proof — file:line, URL of an official source, a measured value, or a posed calculation — found THIS SESSION, or carries the label [non vérifié]. No exceptions.**\n\nSources by domain:\n\n- **Code**: `rg -n \"<symbol|route|table|key>\"` across the whole repo — every call site, not just the edited file. Hyrum's law: who depends on the CURRENT undocumented behavior (output format, ordering, timing, error text)? Does the new code re-read data written by the old code?\n- **Business / life / admin**: read the actual documents (contracts, statements, notes, Memory files); web-search official sources for external facts (rules, prices, deadlines, opening days); compute the real numbers and dates — count, never estimate.\n- **Physical / creative (3D, drawing, video, print)**: open the actual source files (scene, layers, footage); check the official specs (software docs, platform limits, physical medium constraints); measure, don't eyeball.\n- **People**: who else is affected, who must agree, whose workflow breaks if this changes?\n\nIf the facts can't be reached, label the whole map \"non vérifié / spéculatif\". Never present a guess as a finding.\n\n## Step 3: Impact surface (12 universal axes, one killer question each)\n\n1. **Existing state**: does what already exists (data, commitments, acquired rights) stay valid? Partial overwrite possible?\n2. **Conflicts / concurrency**: two actors or processes on the same resource at once — then what?\n3. **Limits & deadlines**: which cap (budget, size, rate, date, capacity) gets hit first, and what happens at the boundary?\n4. **Security / legal**: who can trigger or abuse this? Compliant with law, terms, licenses?\n5. **Partial failure**: stopped halfway — what state remains, is it recoverable?\n6. **Backward compatibility**: old data, clients, habits, commitments still work?\n7. **Scale & durability**: still fine at 100x the volume, or repeated weekly for a year?\n8. **Recurring cost**: money, time, energy that scales with usage?\n9. **Observability**: if it fails silently, how and when do you find out?\n10. **Edge cases**: empty/first-run, oversized, duplicate, offline; holiday, sick day, exception case.\n11. **Checks**: a test or verification that catches the regression BEFORE real impact?\n12. **Reversibility**: one action to undo? Does what got engaged meanwhile (money sent, word given, data written) survive?\n\nKeep only the applicable axes, expressed in the domain's own units and vocabulary. Dismiss the rest in one line (\"N/A: ...\"), never pad.\n\n## Step 4: Premortem (narrative, not checklist)\n\n\"Three weeks later it failed, and it was THIS decision. Write the most plausible incident, then the second.\" Use THIS domain's high-frequency classics — code: off-by-one, empty/first-run state, stale cache, non-idempotent retry, timezone/encoding, two concurrent writers; admin/life: missed deadline, missing document, misread eligibility rule, transfer delay, closed office; business: nobody pays, no distribution channel; 3D/art/print: wrong units or scale, topology breaks at rig, export color shift, asset license.\n\n## Step 5: Rank, cut, decide\n\n- Rank each risk: severity (cosmetic → irreversible loss) x likelihood. Irreversible bumps severity one level.\n- Keep the top 5 maximum. Dismissed risks get one summary line total.\n- Every kept risk carries its **proof** (per Step 2) and **one concrete check** to confirm or kill it: a command, a test, a call, a calculation, a measurement.\n\n## Output format (compact, French by default on this account)\n\n```\nDécision/changement : 1 à 3 lignes.\nAngles morts (4 lectures) : 1 ligne chacune.\n| # | Risque | Preuve | Sév x Prob | Vérification |\nÉcartés : 1 ligne.\nQuestions ouvertes : numérotées, chacune avec un défaut recommandé (accord par silence).\nVerdict : GO / GO avec corrections (listées) / NO-GO + correction minimale.\n```\n\n## Stop conditions and anti-goals\n\n- **ONE pass.** Re-run only if the facts changed afterwards, and only on the delta. Never apply this skill to its own output.\n- It audits ONE stated decision or change. It does not generate unsolicited master plans.\n- Not a substitute for tests, CI, or licensed professionals on high-stakes legal, medical or financial moves: it catches known failure classes, not novel ones. Say that plainly instead of implying completeness.\n\n</skill>"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019f6085-288d-7eb0-ad41-d89410fe11e1"}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-07-14T12:06:09.741Z","ordinal":12,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"<skill>\n<name>zenray-git-bulk-safety</name>\n<path>/Users/zenray/.codex/skills/zenray-git-bulk-safety/SKILL.md</path>\n---\nname: zenray-git-bulk-safety\ndescription: Safely repair, migrate, reindex, or bulk-upload ZenRayProgress media and manifests with Git transport instead of GitHub REST/raw requests. Use for thumbnail rollouts, shard migrations, bulk manifest changes, re-ingestion, or any operation touching more than a few GitHub files.\n---\n\n# ZenRay Git Bulk Safety\n\nUse this skill for a **bulk** ZenRayProgress media or manifest operation. Its invariant is simple: GitHub's REST API is control-plane only; Git is the data plane for large work.\n\n## Choose the transport\n\n| Work | Transport |\n|---|---|\n| One interactive browser capture | Existing app API transaction; the browser and Vercel cannot safely run the user's `gh`/Git credentials. Keep it small and bounded. |\n| Create an empty shard repository | `gh repo create` once if required, then clone/push with Git. |\n| Read, repair, move, reindex, or patch many manifests | Local clone + `git pull`, local files, `git commit`, `git push`. |\n| Push many media binaries | Local Git clone/worktree; batches around 900 MB, a new shard before 4.3 GB. |\n\nNever use `gh api`, `curl`/`fetch` to `api.github.com`, `raw.githubusercontent.com`, or GitHub Contents APIs in a per-file loop. `gh api` is REST despite its name. jsDelivr is allowed only for a small post-push sample of immutable public assets, never for enumerating manifests.\n\n## Mandatory workflow\n\n1. **Protect concurrent work.** Inspect `git status --short`, worktrees, and running jobs. Do not touch another agent's dirty files or a currently active batch.\n2. **Read locally.** Reuse the existing `zenray-data` clone when present; otherwise clone/sparse-clone it with Git. Set non-interactive Git (`GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0`, `GIT_ASKPASS=/usr/bin/true`) and run `git pull --ff-only` before analysis.\n3. **Prove the association before writing.** Create a local table: source path, manifest path, stable identity (content hash where possible), dimensions/size, old pointer, new pointer, and action. Reject collisions and unmatched entries. Names alone are insufficient when duplicates exist.\n4. **Make the operation resumable.** Use deterministic shard paths, a checkpoint/report outside the content tree, and idempotent tests: an already-correct manifest creates no diff; an existing blob need not be uploaded again.\n5. **Commit coherently.** Stage only the operation's files, run `git diff --check`, make an intentionally generic commit message (no personal paths/names), then `git push`. On a concurrent rejection, `git pull --rebase` and retry a bounded number of times.\n6. **Verify the exact result.** Use `git show HEAD:<manifest>` for every changed schema type, `git status --short`, and one or a few pinned jsDelivr checks per shard commit. For encrypted thumbnails, verify the stored URL, IV, and declared dimensions—not just that an HTTP request returns 200.\n\n## ZenRay image invariants\n\n- New public images retain the original encrypted bytes in `cdnChunks[]` (each chunk <=19 MB) and carry `cdnRepo`, `cdnCommit`, `alg`, and `iv`.\n- Their derived WebP uses short edge <=1080 px, quality 60, no upscale. It has its **own plaintext hash**, encrypted blob, pinned `thumb` URL, `thumbIv`, `thumbW`, and `thumbH`.\n- A `cdnChunks[]` manifest with `thumbIv` but no `thumb` is invalid: there is no `cdnPath` from which the viewer can derive the thumbnail address. Repair it in the local clone, then Git commit/push.\n- A viewer never needs ffmpeg or Vercel conversion to render a correct uploaded thumbnail: the browser creates the WebP once at capture time; production only decrypts and displays it from jsDelivr.\n\n## Stop conditions\n\nStop before writes if the association table has a collision, a manifest has no verifiable original/source identity, a shard size would exceed 4.3 GB, Git authentication cannot push, or another active process owns the same files. Report the exact evidence and request direction rather than guessing.\n\n</skill>"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019f6085-288d-7eb0-ad41-d89410fe11e1"}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-07-14T12:06:09.741Z","ordinal":13,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"<skill>\n<name>zenrayshards-upload-media</name>\n<path>/Users/zenray/.codex/skills/zenrayshards-upload-media/SKILL.md</path>\n---\nname: zenrayshards-upload-media\ndescription: \"Skill UNIQUE d'upload média ZenRayProgress — détecte automatiquement l'entrée et route vers le bon pipeline. FICHIERS LOCAUX (vidéo/image, mélangés OK) vers ZenRayShards v2 (vid=privé+chiffré+hvc1, img=public jsDelivr) ; LIEN YouTube vers nœud .media.json type:'youtube' ou audioOnly:true. Pour cent fichiers ou plus, réparation, migration, réindexation ou modification de manifestes, impose d'abord zenray-git-bulk-safety : clone/pull/commit/push Git, jamais REST/raw dans une boucle. Déclencheurs — upload zenrayshards, upload vid zenrayshards, uploade ce(s) fichier(s)/cette vidéo/cette musique, tout chemin local ou lien youtube.com/youtu.be avec intention d'ajout au Create/Drive. Si le dossier Create cible manque, DEMANDER à l'utilisateur, jamais deviner. Remplace et absorbe les anciens skills zenray-upload-media, zenray-upload-video, zenray-upload-audio (supprimés le 2026-07-06).\"\n---\n\n# zenrayshards-upload-media — LE skill d'upload unique (fichiers locaux + liens YouTube)\n\nFusion définitive (2026-07-06, décision Matthieu) des 4 anciens skills d'upload en UN SEUL point\nd'entrée, aligné sur l'architecture ZenRayShards v2 du 2026-07-05/06. Repo :\n`/Users/zenray/Create/Build/Productions/GithubProductions/ZenRayProgress`.\n\n**AVANT TOUT UPLOAD, lire `docs/INDEX-ERREURS-FEATURES.md` du repo** (index court des bugs déjà payés\net des règles dures) — c'est la mémoire permanente du projet, elle prime sur toute improvisation.\n\n## Garde obligatoire : petit upload ou opération de masse\n\n1. Compter les fichiers et classer l'action **avant** de lancer un script.\n2. **Moins de 100 fichiers, ajout direct uniquement** : les runners ci-dessous sont autorisés. Ils ont encore\n   quelques appels REST bornés (skip + écriture des manifestes), acceptables pour un geste interactif court.\n3. **100 fichiers ou plus**, ou toute réparation/migration/déplacement/régénération/réindexation de manifestes :\n   lire et appliquer `$zenray-git-bulk-safety` AVANT toute écriture. Réutiliser ou créer un clone sparse de\n   `zenray-data`, faire l'inventaire et les skips sur disque, écrire localement, puis `git commit` + `git push`.\n   Aucun `gh api`, `api.github.com`, `raw.githubusercontent.com` ou Contents API dans une boucle.\n4. `gh auth token` peut seulement fournir l'authentification au transport Git et `gh repo create` peut créer\n   UN shard neuf. **`gh api` est REST** : il est interdit pour les données ou manifestes de masse.\n5. Si le runner ciblé ne sait pas encore faire cette passe Git-only, NE PAS le lancer sur un gros lot : arrêter,\n   expliquer l'écart et corriger/choisir le pipeline Git avant de consommer le quota.\n\n## Étape 1 — router l'entrée (3 cas, détection automatique)\n\n| Entrée fournie par Matthieu | Cas | Pipeline |\n|---|---|---|\n| Chemin(s) local(aux) dont `kindForName()` = video | A | `scripts/upload-vidraw-list.mjs` (ZenRayShards v2 privé) |\n| Chemin(s) local(aux) dont `kindForName()` = image / image-lib | B | `scripts/upload-img-list.mjs` (ZenRayShards v2 public jsDelivr) |\n| Lien(s) youtube.com / youtu.be | C | `src/lib/youtubeLink.js` (nœud référence, zéro stockage) |\n\nDétection de type : TOUJOURS `kindForName()` de `src/lib/fileKinds.js` (source unique upload+viewer),\njamais une liste d'extensions ad hoc. Lot mixte local (A+B) : lancer les deux pipelines l'un après\nl'autre vers le même `<dest>`, rapporter le résultat consolidé. Cas C avec mots \"musique\"/\"piste\"/\n\"chanson\"/\"OST\" -> `audioOnly:true` (widget compact YouTube Music) ; sinon vidéo 16:9 classique.\nFichier ni vidéo ni image ni lien YouTube (audio local, PDF, zip...) : SIGNALER explicitement (aucun\npipeline d'aperçu n'existe pour eux ici), ne jamais deviner ni ignorer en silence.\n\n## Règles dures v2 (mémoire permanente, ne JAMAIS y déroger)\n\n1. **Shard neuf seulement après inventaire local non vide** : `zenrayshards-<type>-<hex12>` (nom aléatoire\n   façon workflow). Aucun check de place distant par média : le compteur local ouvre le shard suivant avant\n   ~4,3 Go. Ne pas cloner un ancien shard binaire ; pour les manifestes `zenray-data`, un clone sparse Git\n   est au contraire obligatoire dès que l'opération est de masse.\n2. **Visibilité par convention de nom** : `zenrayshards-img-*` = PUBLIC (jsDelivr l'exige) ; tout\n   autre type (vid/blob/pdf/doc) = PRIVÉ, lu via api.github.com Accept:raw + token (`/api/raw-token`).\n3. **hvc1 obligatoire** : tout remux HEVC->MP4 porte `-tag:v hvc1` (déjà dans `remuxFaststart()` —\n   sans lui, hev1 = illisible iPhone/Apple ; bug payé le 2026-07-05). h264 non concerné.\n4. **Ne JAMAIS recréer un repo avec un ancien nom** (zenray-vidraw-NN, zenray-img-public-NN, 313\n   renommés le 2026-07-06) : les vieux manifestes vivent sur la redirection GitHub ancien->nouveau.\n5. **Chiffrement AES-256-GCM par part** (vidéo), clé maître unique (`.zenray-media-key` /\n   `ZENRAY_MEDIA_KEY`) — jamais régénérer une clé existante.\n6. Conteneur ISO-BMFF `.mp4/.mov/.m4v` : conservé tel quel s'il est déjà faststart ; sinon remux lossless\n   vers MP4. Autres conteneurs (mkv/avi/webm...) : remux auto vers .mp4 en stream-copy (aucun réencodage).\n   Seule la 1re piste vidéo + 1re piste audio survivent ; sous-titres embarqués perdus -> vérifier\n   `ffprobe` avant d'uploader un fichier multi-pistes (hardsub via skill `anime-hardsub-hevc` sinon).\n\n## Cas A — vidéos locales -> ZenRayShards v2 (ajout direct <100)\n\n> `upload-vidraw-list.mjs` utilise encore REST pour le skip et les manifestes. À partir de 100 vidéos,\n> ne pas l'exécuter tel quel : basculer vers le workflow Git-only ci-dessus avant l'upload.\n\n```bash\ncd /Users/zenray/Create/Build/Productions/GithubProductions/ZenRayProgress\nGITHUB_TOKEN=$(gh auth token) node scripts/upload-vidraw-list.mjs \"<dest>\" \"<vid1>\" \"<vid2>\" ...\n```\nTrie court->long, saute les vidéos déjà en ligne (relance sûre), manifeste committé PAR vidéo (chaque\népisode visible au fil de l'eau), clone local nettoyé en fin de lot.\n\n**Run long (>500 Mo ou plusieurs vidéos) -> fond :**\n```bash\nmkdir -p ~/.zr-runs\nGITHUB_TOKEN=$(gh auth token) nohup node scripts/upload-vidraw-list.mjs \\\n  \"<dest>\" \"<fichiers...>\" > ~/.zr-runs/vidraw-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M).log 2>&1 &\ndisown\n```\n**Suivi : TOUJOURS `node scripts/vidraw-progress.mjs`** (% réel en octets + vidéo en cours), jamais\nun simple tail. Jamais de boucle de sleeps courts.\n\n## Cas B — images locales -> ZenRayShards v2 (public jsDelivr, ajout direct <100)\n\n> `upload-img-list.mjs` utilise encore REST pour le skip et les commits de manifestes. À partir de 100 images,\n> ne pas l'exécuter tel quel : basculer vers le workflow Git-only ci-dessus avant l'upload.\n\n```bash\ncd /Users/zenray/Create/Build/Productions/GithubProductions/ZenRayProgress\nGITHUB_TOKEN=$(gh auth token) node scripts/upload-img-list.mjs \"<dest>\" \"<img1>\" \"<img2>\" ...\n```\nChiffré (IV déterministe = dédup du blob), vignette WebP 1080px/q90 auto (sans upscale, avec\n`thumb`/`thumbIv`/`thumbW`/`thumbH` ; standard THUMB_QUALITY_PCT centralisé dans src/config/thumbnail.js,\nSEUL fichier à éditer pour changer la qualité partout — audit SSIM 2026-07-14, q100 interdit). **DÉCOUPAGE EN MORCEAUX ≤19 Mo\n(2026-07-07)** : jsDelivr refuse de servir un fichier > ~20 Mo — au lieu d'un dérivé recompressé,\nl'original chiffré est découpé en morceaux ≤19 Mo (`cdnChunks[]`, TOUJOURS présent, même 1 seul\nélément pour une petite image — logique uniforme, aucun cas particulier par taille). Le client\nreconstitue en concaténant les morceaux dans l'ordre avant de déchiffrer : l'image affichée/téléchargée\nEST l'original bit-exact, jamais recompressée, et sert TOUJOURS via jsDelivr (raw.github plus utilisé\npour les nouveaux uploads image — c'était l'ancien schéma, `cdnPath` mono-fichier, qui consommait le\nquota raw.github à CHAQUE vue plein écran). Par défaut, le script ingère des lots d’environ 900 Mo, pousse\nune fois puis committe les manifestes par tranches de 200 ; `ZR_IMG_BATCH_MB=0` rétablit la visibilité\nimage par image, plus lente. Après chaque lot, il vérifie une URL représentative par couple `(repo, commit)` :\ncomme le commit jsDelivr est immuable, ce test valide que l’arbre correspondant est servi sans multiplier\nles requêtes pour chaque morceau. Depuis le 2026-07-11, le script saute une image dont le manifeste exact existe\ndéjà : relancer le même lot après interruption est sûr et ne crée plus de tuile en double. Le skip est basé\nsur `<dest>/<nom original>.img.json`, pas sur le hash : pour remplacer un fichier MODIFIÉ qui garde le même\nnom, ne pas compter sur la relance — déplacer/supprimer volontairement l'ancien manifeste ou changer le nom.\nImages déjà en ligne AVANT le 2026-07-07 (ancien schéma `cdnPath`) : jamais migrées, toujours lues via\nraw.github comme avant — décision Matthieu, aucune urgence à les convertir.\n\n## Cas C — liens YouTube (vidéo OU audio) -> nœud référence, zéro stockage\n\nPrérequis : serveur local `:3000` (`zsh scripts/dev-server.sh`, vérifier `curl -s -o /dev/null -w\n\"%{http_code}\" http://localhost:3000/` = 200).\n\n```bash\ncd /Users/zenray/Create/Build/Productions/GithubProductions/ZenRayProgress\nnode --input-type=module -e \"\nimport { createYoutubeLinkNode, createYoutubeLinkNodesBatch } from './src/lib/youtubeLink.js'\n// UNE vidéo :   await createYoutubeLinkNode('<url>', '<dest>')\n// UNE musique : await createYoutubeLinkNode('<url>', '<dest>', { audioOnly: true })\n// LOT (un lien par ligne) : await createYoutubeLinkNodesBatch(text, '<dest>', null, { audioOnly: true|false })\nconst res = await createYoutubeLinkNode('URL_ICI', 'pages/DEST_ICI')\nconsole.log(JSON.stringify(res, null, 2))\n\"\n```\nTitre/auteur/vignette via oEmbed public (jamais bloquant : repli sur l'ID brut si indispo). Batch =\nséquentiel + 1 seul commit d'index. `audioOnly:true` -> widget compact façon YouTube Music\n(transport shuffle/prev/play/next/repeat, téléchargement MP3 via yt-dlp en LOCAL uniquement).\n\n## Cherchable dans la searchbar (correctif 2026-07-14, plus jamais à faire à la main pour les images)\n\n**Bug trouvé par Matthieu** : les images uploadées via `upload-img-list.mjs` / `upload-img-list-git.mjs`\nn'apparaissaient PAS dans la recherche du site. Ce n'était PAS un problème de champ `tags` (absent de toute\nla logique de scoring — vérifié par grep exhaustif : la recherche matche sur le CHEMIN normalisé + le nom,\njamais sur `tags`). Le vrai mécanisme : un fichier n'est cherchable que s'il est dans l'index public\n(`zenray-index-public/delta.json`, chiffré) OU dans le gros index reconstruit à la main\n(`build-search-index.mjs --apply`, jamais automatique). L'app appelle ce mécanisme après chaque drop/Ctrl-V\n(`action=index-add` sur `/api/page`) ; **les scripts CLI ne l'appelaient jamais**.\n\n**Corrigé** : `upload-img-list.mjs` et `upload-img-list-git.mjs` appellent maintenant automatiquement\n`appendPublicDelta`/`thumbsForPaths` (ré-exportées de `server/page.js`) après leur commit zenray-data —\nchaque image uploadée par CES DEUX runners est cherchable immédiatement, sans action manuelle. Best-effort\n(un échec n'invalide jamais l'upload lui-même, juste rattrapable).\n\n**Rattrapage manuel si besoin** (déjà en ligne mais pas indexé, ou runner qui a loggé `⚠ index-add échoué`) :\n```bash\nGITHUB_TOKEN=$(gh auth token) node scripts/index-add-paths.mjs --dir \"pages/<dossier du site>\"\n```\n\n**Corrigé aussi pour la vidéo (2026-07-14, même jour)** : `upload-vidraw-list.mjs` (Cas A) appelle désormais\nle même `appendPublicDelta`/`thumbsForPaths` après chaque vidéo uploadée — plus aucun trou : **TOUT média\n(image ou vidéo) uploadé via ce skill est cherchable immédiatement, sans étape manuelle, pour toujours**.\nVignette du delta vide pour la vidéo (`thumbsForPaths` ne résout que `.img.json`) : le CHEMIN est cherchable\ntout de suite, la vignette suit au prochain rebuild complet — cosmétique, pas fonctionnel.\n\n**2e piège, plus vicieux, trouvé en VÉRIFIANT ce premier fix (pas en le supposant bon)** : `appendPublicDelta`\nlit son état courant via `raw.githubusercontent.com`, qui a son propre cache CDN ~5 min (documenté dans\nserver/page.js). La toute première version de ce correctif appelait `appendPublicDelta` UNE FOIS PAR LOT de\n200 chemins — pour un épisode de 300-400 frames (le cas normal), ça fait 2 appels séquentiels rapprochés qui\nlisent le MÊME instantané périmé et s'ÉCRASENT l'un l'autre au lieu de s'accumuler. Un rattrapage sur 4949\nchemins annonçait \"4949/4949 OK\" alors que le delta réel n'en contenait que 518 — le code retour \"ok\" ne\nprouve RIEN sur un système avec cache, seule une relecture directe (déchiffrer delta.json, ou tester une\nvraie recherche dans le navigateur) le prouve. Corrigé pour de bon : `appendPublicDelta` retry en interne sur\nun ref-update rejeté (server/page.js, même patron que sa fonction sœur `commitTree`) ET, plus important, les\n4 scripts (3 uploaders + index-add-paths.mjs) résolvent les vignettes par lots de 200 mais n'appellent\n`appendPublicDelta` qu'**UNE SEULE FOIS** avec la liste complète du run — zéro lecture-modification-écriture\nrépétée, donc zéro risque d'écrasement. Vérifié par lecture directe du blob chiffré (5033 chemins, HOTD=3701\nexact) ET par recherche réelle dans le navigateur (dev local) après propagation du cache (~5-10 min).\n\n## Vérification post-upload (tous cas)\n\n```bash\ncurl -s \"http://localhost:3000/api/page?action=children&dir=<dest>\" | python3 -m json.tool\n```\nTuile visible + lecture au clic. Pour le stockage d'un fichier précis : clic droit -> « Stockage\n(Index) » dans l'app, ou la vue Index de la sidebar (repos, parts, tailles).\n\nPour une opération Git-only de masse, ne pas utiliser cette API locale pour inventorier ou valider chaque\nfichier : vérifier les manifestes dans le clone (`git show HEAD:<chemin>`), `git diff --check`, puis une URL\njsDelivr épinglée représentative par commit de shard. Ouvrir ensuite quelques tuiles réelles dans le navigateur.\n\n## Gestion d'erreur\n\n- `✗ ÉCHEC <fichier>` : lire le log complet avant toute relance, jamais de boucle aveugle.\n- Un lot >=100 qui appelle encore REST : STOP. Ne pas « essayer quand même » ni fractionner artificiellement\n  pour contourner le garde-fou ; appliquer `$zenray-git-bulk-safety` et reprendre depuis l'inventaire local.\n- Clé maître introuvable : la récupérer (`Identity/Matthieu1.md`, repo Memory) — JAMAIS en générer.\n- Crash à mi-lot vidéo : relancer la même commande (skip auto) ; parts orphelines inoffensives.\n- Lien YouTube non reconnu : formats acceptés youtu.be/<id>, watch?v=, /embed/, /shorts/.\n- Lecture privée morte en prod : vérifier que le code du drive a été saisi (le SW reçoit le secret\n  via la page — bug résolu le 2026-07-06, cf. INDEX-ERREURS-FEATURES).\n\n</skill>"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019f6085-288d-7eb0-ad41-d89410fe11e1"}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-07-14T12:06:09.741Z","ordinal":14,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"<skill>\n<name>skill-creator</name>\n<path>/Users/zenray/.codex/skills/.system/skill-creator/SKILL.md</path>\n---\nname: skill-creator\ndescription: Guide for creating effective skills. This skill should be used when users want to create a new skill (or update an existing skill) that extends Codex's capabilities with specialized knowledge, workflows, or tool integrations.\nmetadata:\n  short-description: Create or update a skill\n---\n\n# Skill Creator\n\nThis skill provides guidance for creating effective skills.\n\n## About Skills\n\nSkills are modular, self-contained folders that extend Codex's capabilities by providing\nspecialized knowledge, workflows, and tools. Think of them as \"onboarding guides\" for specific\ndomains or tasks—they transform Codex from a general-purpose agent into a specialized agent\nequipped with procedural knowledge that no model can fully possess.\n\n### What Skills Provide\n\n1. Specialized workflows - Multi-step procedures for specific domains\n2. Tool integrations - Instructions for working with specific file formats or APIs\n3. Domain expertise - Company-specific knowledge, schemas, business logic\n4. Bundled resources - Scripts, references, and assets for complex and repetitive tasks\n\n## Core Principles\n\n### Concise is Key\n\nThe context window is a public good. Skills share the context window with everything else Codex needs: system prompt, conversation history, other Skills' metadata, and the actual user request.\n\n**Default assumption: Codex is already very smart.** Only add context Codex doesn't already have. Challenge each piece of information: \"Does Codex really need this explanation?\" and \"Does this paragraph justify its token cost?\"\n\nPrefer concise examples over verbose explanations.\n\n### Set Appropriate Degrees of Freedom\n\nMatch the level of specificity to the task's fragility and variability:\n\n**High freedom (text-based instructions)**: Use when multiple approaches are valid, decisions depend on context, or heuristics guide the approach.\n\n**Medium freedom (pseudocode or scripts with parameters)**: Use when a preferred pattern exists, some variation is acceptable, or configuration affects behavior.\n\n**Low freedom (specific scripts, few parameters)**: Use when operations are fragile and error-prone, consistency is critical, or a specific sequence must be followed.\n\nThink of Codex as exploring a path: a narrow bridge with cliffs needs specific guardrails (low freedom), while an open field allows many routes (high freedom).\n\n### Protect Validation Integrity\n\nYou may use subagents during iteration to validate whether a skill works on realistic tasks or whether a suspected problem is real. This is most useful when you want an independent pass on the skill's behavior, outputs, or failure modes after a revision.  Only do this when it is possible to start new subagents.\n\nWhen using subagents for validation, treat that as an evaluation surface. The goal is to learn whether the skill generalizes, not whether another agent can reconstruct the answer from leaked context.\n\nPrefer raw artifacts such as example prompts, outputs, diffs, logs, or traces. Give the minimum task-local context needed to perform the validation. Avoid passing the intended answer, suspected bug, intended fix, or your prior conclusions unless the validation explicitly requires them.\n\n### Anatomy of a Skill\n\nEvery skill consists of a required SKILL.md file and optional bundled resources:\n\n```\nskill-name/\n├── SKILL.md (required)\n│   ├── YAML frontmatter metadata (required)\n│   │   ├── name: (required)\n│   │   └── description: (required)\n│   └── Markdown instructions (required)\n├── agents/ (recommended)\n│   └── openai.yaml - UI metadata for skill lists and chips\n└── Bundled Resources (optional)\n    ├── scripts/          - Executable code (Python/Bash/etc.)\n    ├── references/       - Documentation intended to be loaded into context as needed\n    └── assets/           - Files used in output (templates, icons, fonts, etc.)\n```\n\n#### SKILL.md (required)\n\nEvery SKILL.md consists of:\n\n- **Frontmatter** (YAML): Contains `name` and `description` fields. These are the only fields that Codex reads to determine when the skill gets used, thus it is very important to be clear and comprehensive in describing what the skill is, and when it should be used.\n- **Body** (Markdown): Instructions and guidance for using the skill. Only loaded AFTER the skill triggers (if at all).\n\n#### Agents metadata (recommended)\n\n- UI-facing metadata for skill lists and chips\n- Read references/openai_yaml.md before generating values and follow its descriptions and constraints\n- Create: human-facing `display_name`, `short_description`, and `default_prompt` by reading the skill\n- Generate deterministically by passing the values as `--interface key=value` to `scripts/generate_openai_yaml.py` or `scripts/init_skill.py`\n- On updates: validate `agents/openai.yaml` still matches SKILL.md; regenerate if stale\n- Only include other optional interface fields (icons, brand color) if explicitly provided\n- See references/openai_yaml.md for field definitions and examples\n\n#### Bundled Resources (optional)\n\n##### Scripts (`scripts/`)\n\nExecutable code (Python/Bash/etc.) for tasks that require deterministic reliability or are repeatedly rewritten.\n\n- **When to include**: When the same code is being rewritten repeatedly or deterministic reliability is needed\n- **Example**: `scripts/rotate_pdf.py` for PDF rotation tasks\n- **Benefits**: Token efficient, deterministic, may be executed without loading into context\n- **Note**: Scripts may still need to be read by Codex for patching or environment-specific adjustments\n\n##### References (`references/`)\n\nDocumentation and reference material intended to be loaded as needed into context to inform Codex's process and thinking.\n\n- **When to include**: For documentation that Codex should reference while working\n- **Examples**: `references/finance.md` for financial schemas, `references/mnda.md` for company NDA template, `references/policies.md` for company policies, `references/api_docs.md` for API specifications\n- **Use cases**: Database schemas, API documentation, domain knowledge, company policies, detailed workflow guides\n- **Benefits**: Keeps SKILL.md lean, loaded only when Codex determines it's needed\n- **Best practice**: If files are large (>10k words), include grep search patterns in SKILL.md\n- **Avoid duplication**: Information should live in either SKILL.md or references files, not both. Prefer references files for detailed information unless it's truly core to the skill—this keeps SKILL.md lean while making information discoverable without hogging the context window. Keep only essential procedural instructions and workflow guidance in SKILL.md; move detailed reference material, schemas, and examples to references files.\n\n##### Assets (`assets/`)\n\nFiles not intended to be loaded into context, but rather used within the output Codex produces.\n\n- **When to include**: When the skill needs files that will be used in the final output\n- **Examples**: `assets/logo.png` for brand assets, `assets/slides.pptx` for PowerPoint templates, `assets/frontend-template/` for HTML/React boilerplate, `assets/font.ttf` for typography\n- **Use cases**: Templates, images, icons, boilerplate code, fonts, sample documents that get copied or modified\n- **Benefits**: Separates output resources from documentation, enables Codex to use files without loading them into context\n\n#### What to Not Include in a Skill\n\nA skill should only contain essential files that directly support its functionality. Do NOT create extraneous documentation or auxiliary files, including:\n\n- README.md\n- INSTALLATION_GUIDE.md\n- QUICK_REFERENCE.md\n- CHANGELOG.md\n- etc.\n\nThe skill should only contain the information needed for an AI agent to do the job at hand. It should not contain auxiliary context about the process that went into creating it, setup and testing procedures, user-facing documentation, etc. Creating additional documentation files just adds clutter and confusion.\n\n### Progressive Disclosure Design Principle\n\nSkills use a three-level loading system to manage context efficiently:\n\n1. **Metadata (name + description)** - Always in context (~100 words)\n2. **SKILL.md body** - When skill triggers (<5k words)\n3. **Bundled resources** - As needed by Codex (Unlimited because scripts can be executed without reading into context window)\n\n#### Progressive Disclosure Patterns\n\nKeep SKILL.md body to the essentials and under 500 lines to minimize context bloat. Split content into separate files when approaching this limit. When splitting out content into other files, it is very important to reference them from SKILL.md and describe clearly when to read them, to ensure the reader of the skill knows they exist and when to use them.\n\n**Key principle:** When a skill supports multiple variations, frameworks, or options, keep only the core workflow and selection guidance in SKILL.md. Move variant-specific details (patterns, examples, configuration) into separate reference files.\n\n**Pattern 1: High-level guide with references**\n\n```markdown\n# PDF Processing\n\n## Quick start\n\nExtract text with pdfplumber:\n[code example]\n\n## Advanced features\n\n- **Form filling**: See [FORMS.md](FORMS.md) for complete guide\n- **API reference**: See [REFERENCE.md](REFERENCE.md) for all methods\n- **Examples**: See [EXAMPLES.md](EXAMPLES.md) for common patterns\n```\n\nCodex loads FORMS.md, REFERENCE.md, or EXAMPLES.md only when needed.\n\n**Pattern 2: Domain-specific organization**\n\nFor Skills with multiple domains, organize content by domain to avoid loading irrelevant context:\n\n```\nbigquery-skill/\n├── SKILL.md (overview and navigation)\n└── reference/\n    ├── finance.md (revenue, billing metrics)\n    ├── sales.md (opportunities, pipeline)\n    ├── product.md (API usage, features)\n    └── marketing.md (campaigns, attribution)\n```\n\nWhen a user asks about sales metrics, Codex only reads sales.md.\n\nSimilarly, for skills supporting multiple frameworks or variants, organize by variant:\n\n```\ncloud-deploy/\n├── SKILL.md (workflow + provider selection)\n└── references/\n    ├── aws.md (AWS deployment patterns)\n    ├── gcp.md (GCP deployment patterns)\n    └── azure.md (Azure deployment patterns)\n```\n\nWhen the user chooses AWS, Codex only reads aws.md.\n\n**Pattern 3: Conditional details**\n\nShow basic content, link to advanced content:\n\n```markdown\n# DOCX Processing\n\n## Creating documents\n\nUse docx-js for new documents. See [DOCX-JS.md](DOCX-JS.md).\n\n## Editing documents\n\nFor simple edits, modify the XML directly.\n\n**For tracked changes**: See [REDLINING.md](REDLINING.md)\n**For OOXML details**: See [OOXML.md](OOXML.md)\n```\n\nCodex reads REDLINING.md or OOXML.md only when the user needs those features.\n\n**Important guidelines:**\n\n- **Avoid deeply nested references** - Keep references one level deep from SKILL.md. All reference files should link directly from SKILL.md.\n- **Structure longer reference files** - For files longer than 100 lines, include a table of contents at the top so Codex can see the full scope when previewing.\n\n## Skill Creation Process\n\nSkill creation involves these steps:\n\n1. Understand the skill with concrete examples\n2. Plan reusable skill contents (scripts, references, assets)\n3. Initialize the skill (run init_skill.py)\n4. Edit the skill (implement resources and write SKILL.md)\n5. Validate the skill (run quick_validate.py)\n6. Iterate based on real usage and forward-test complex skills.\n\nFollow these steps in order, skipping only if there is a clear reason why they are not applicable.\n\n### Skill Naming\n\n- Use lowercase letters, digits, and hyphens only; normalize user-provided titles to hyphen-case (e.g., \"Plan Mode\" -> `plan-mode`).\n- When generating names, generate a name under 64 characters (letters, digits, hyphens).\n- Prefer short, verb-led phrases that describe the action.\n- Namespace by tool when it improves clarity or triggering (e.g., `gh-address-comments`, `linear-address-issue`).\n- Name the skill folder exactly after the skill name.\n\n### Step 1: Understanding the Skill with Concrete Examples\n\nSkip this step only when the skill's usage patterns are already clearly understood. It remains valuable even when working with an existing skill.\n\nTo create an effective skill, clearly understand concrete examples of how the skill will be used. This understanding can come from either direct user examples or generated examples that are validated with user feedback.\n\nFor example, when building an image-editor skill, relevant questions include:\n\n- \"What functionality should the image-editor skill support? Editing, rotating, anything else?\"\n- \"Can you give some examples of how this skill would be used?\"\n- \"I can imagine users asking for things like 'Remove the red-eye from this image' or 'Rotate this image'. Are there other ways you imagine this skill being used?\"\n- \"What would a user say that should trigger this skill?\"\n- \"Where should I create this skill? If you do not have a preference, I will place it in `$CODEX_HOME/skills` (or `~/.codex/skills` when `CODEX_HOME` is unset) so Codex can discover it automatically.\"\n\nTo avoid overwhelming users, avoid asking too many questions in a single message. Start with the most important questions and follow up as needed for better effectiveness.\n\nConclude this step when there is a clear sense of the functionality the skill should support.\n\n### Step 2: Planning the Reusable Skill Contents\n\nTo turn concrete examples into an effective skill, analyze each example by:\n\n1. Considering how to execute on the example from scratch\n2. Identifying what scripts, references, and assets would be helpful when executing these workflows repeatedly\n\nExample: When building a `pdf-editor` skill to handle queries like \"Help me rotate this PDF,\" the analysis shows:\n\n1. Rotating a PDF requires re-writing the same code each time\n2. A `scripts/rotate_pdf.py` script would be helpful to store in the skill\n\nExample: When designing a `frontend-webapp-builder` skill for queries like \"Build me a todo app\" or \"Build me a dashboard to track my steps,\" the analysis shows:\n\n1. Writing a frontend webapp requires the same boilerplate HTML/React each time\n2. An `assets/hello-world/` template containing the boilerplate HTML/React project files would be helpful to store in the skill\n\nExample: When building a `big-query` skill to handle queries like \"How many users have logged in today?\" the analysis shows:\n\n1. Querying BigQuery requires re-discovering the table schemas and relationships each time\n2. A `references/schema.md` file documenting the table schemas would be helpful to store in the skill\n\nTo establish the skill's contents, analyze each concrete example to create a list of the reusable resources to include: scripts, references, and assets.\n\n### Step 3: Initializing the Skill\n\nAt this point, it is time to actually create the skill.\n\nSkip this step only if the skill being developed already exists. In this case, continue to the next step.\n\nBefore running `init_skill.py`, ask where the user wants the skill created. If they do not specify a location, default to `$CODEX_HOME/skills`; when `CODEX_HOME` is unset, fall back to `~/.codex/skills` so the skill is auto-discovered.\n\nWhen creating a new skill from scratch, always run the `init_skill.py` script. The script conveniently generates a new template skill directory that automatically includes everything a skill requires, making the skill creation process much more efficient and reliable.\n\nUsage:\n\n```bash\nscripts/init_skill.py <skill-name> --path <output-directory> [--resources scripts,references,assets] [--examples]\n```\n\nExamples:\n\n```bash\nscripts/init_skill.py my-skill --path \"${CODEX_HOME:-$HOME/.codex}/skills\"\nscripts/init_skill.py my-skill --path \"${CODEX_HOME:-$HOME/.codex}/skills\" --resources scripts,references\nscripts/init_skill.py my-skill --path ~/work/skills --resources scripts --examples\n```\n\nThe script:\n\n- Creates the skill directory at the specified path\n- Generates a SKILL.md template with proper frontmatter and TODO placeholders\n- Creates `agents/openai.yaml` using agent-generated `display_name`, `short_description`, and `default_prompt` passed via `--interface key=value`\n- Optionally creates resource directories based on `--resources`\n- Optionally adds example files when `--examples` is set\n\nAfter initialization, customize the SKILL.md and add resources as needed. If you used `--examples`, replace or delete placeholder files.\n\nGenerate `display_name`, `short_description`, and `default_prompt` by reading the skill, then pass them as `--interface key=value` to `init_skill.py` or regenerate with:\n\n```bash\nscripts/generate_openai_yaml.py <path/to/skill-folder> --interface key=value\n```\n\nOnly include other optional interface fields when the user explicitly provides them. For full field descriptions and examples, see references/openai_yaml.md.\n\n### Step 4: Edit the Skill\n\nWhen editing the (newly-generated or existing) skill, remember that the skill is being created for another instance of Codex to use. Include information that would be beneficial and non-obvious to Codex. Consider what procedural knowledge, domain-specific details, or reusable assets would help another Codex instance execute these tasks more effectively.\n\nAfter substantial revisions, or if the skill is particularly tricky, you should use subagents to forward-test the skill on realistic tasks or artifacts. When doing so, pass the artifact under validation rather than your diagnosis of what is wrong, and keep the prompt generic enough that success depends on transferable reasoning rather than hidden ground truth.\n\n#### Start with Reusable Skill Contents\n\nTo begin implementation, start with the reusable resources identified above: `scripts/`, `references/`, and `assets/` files. Note that this step may require user input. For example, when implementing a `brand-guidelines` skill, the user may need to provide brand assets or templates to store in `assets/`, or documentation to store in `references/`.\n\nAdded scripts must be tested by actually running them to ensure there are no bugs and that the output matches what is expected. If there are many similar scripts, only a representative sample needs to be tested to ensure confidence that they all work while balancing time to completion.\n\nIf you used `--examples`, delete any placeholder files that are not needed for the skill. Only create resource directories that are actually required.\n\n#### Update SKILL.md\n\n**Writing Guidelines:** Always use imperative/infinitive form.\n\n##### Frontmatter\n\nWrite the YAML frontmatter with `name` and `description`:\n\n- `name`: The skill name\n- `description`: This is the primary triggering mechanism for your skill, and helps Codex understand when to use the skill.\n  - Include both what the Skill does and specific triggers/contexts for when to use it.\n  - Include all \"when to use\" information here - Not in the body. The body is only loaded after triggering, so \"When to Use This Skill\" sections in the body are not helpful to Codex.\n  - Example description for a `docx` skill: \"Comprehensive document creation, editing, and analysis with support for tracked changes, comments, formatting preservation, and text extraction. Use when Codex needs to work with professional documents (.docx files) for: (1) Creating new documents, (2) Modifying or editing content, (3) Working with tracked changes, (4) Adding comments, or any other document tasks\"\n\nDo not include any other fields in YAML frontmatter.\n\n##### Body\n\nWrite instructions for using the skill and its bundled resources.\n\n### Step 5: Validate the Skill\n\nOnce development of the skill is complete, validate the skill folder to catch basic issues early:\n\n```bash\nscripts/quick_validate.py <path/to/skill-folder>\n```\n\nThe validation script checks YAML frontmatter format, required fields, and naming rules. If validation fails, fix the reported issues and run the command again.\n\n### Step 6: Iterate\n\nAfter testing the skill, you may detect the skill is complex enough that it requires forward-testing; or users may request improvements.\n\nUser testing often this happens right after using the skill, with fresh context of how the skill performed.\n\n**Forward-testing and iteration workflow:**\n\n1. Use the skill on real tasks\n2. Notice struggles or inefficiencies\n3. Identify how SKILL.md or bundled resources should be updated\n4. Implement changes and test again\n5. Forward-test if it is reasonable and appropriate\n\n## Forward-testing\n\nTo forward-test, launch subagents as a way to stress test the skill with minimal context.\nSubagents should *not* know that they are being asked to test the skill.  They should be treated as\nan agent asked to perform a task by the user.  Prompts to subagents should look like:\n  `Use $skill-x at /path/to/skill-x to solve problem y`\nNot:\n  `Review the skill at /path/to/skill-x; pretend a user asks you to...`\n\nDecision rule for forward-testing:\n  - Err on the side of forward-testing\n  - Ask for approval if you think there's a risk that forward-testing would:\n    * take a long time,\n    * require additional approvals from the user, or\n    * modify live production systems\n\n  In these cases, show the user your proposed prompt and request (1) a yes/no decision, and\n  (2) any suggested modifictions.\n\nConsiderations when forward-testing:\n   - use fresh threads for independent passes\n   - pass the skill, and a request in a similar way the user would.\n   - pass raw artifacts, not your conclusions\n   - avoid showing expected answers or intended fixes\n   - rebuild context from source artifacts after each iteration\n   - review the subagent's output and reasoning and emitted artifacts\n   - avoid leaving artifacts the agent can find on disk between iterations;\n     clean up subagents' artifacts to avoid additional contamination.\n\nIf forward-testing only succeeds when subagents see leaked context, tighten the skill or the\nforward-testing setup before trusting the result.\n\n</skill>"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019f6085-288d-7eb0-ad41-d89410fe11e1"}}}
